  "answer": "The key comparison is the **name, idea and overall impression**, not merely whether the word literally describes something shown in the device.\n\n**Act — legal test:** Section 10 asks whether one trade mark so nearly resembles the other that it is likely to deceive or cause confusion. [TMA1995/s10]\n\n**Manual — examination practice:**\n\n1. **Identify the device’s name and idea.** What name and concept would a person of normal intelligence and memory associate with the picture? Compare those with the name and idea conveyed by the word mark. An equivalent picture and word may leave the same overall impression despite their different visual forms. [TMM/Part26/6/6/12]\n\n2. **Check the specificity of that association.** The Manual’s EMU example illustrates a device conveying precisely the word’s name and idea. Its contrasting shark example cautions against assuming that a depiction of a shark necessarily conveys “Great White Shark”. [TMM/Part26/6/6/12]\n\n3. **Compare each mark as a whole.** Where the device mark is a composite containing words and imagery, do not isolate the wording and treat the remaining features as irrelevant. The Manual directs a whole-mark comparison. [TMM/Part26/6/6/8~1]\n\n4. **Assess any shared element in context.** Consider whether it is descriptive of the relevant goods or services. A shared element alone does not establish deceptive similarity, and an element should not too readily be labelled an essential feature. [TMM/Part26/6/6/4~2]\n\n5. **Return to the consumer’s recollection.** Assess how the similarities and differences affect the impression or recollection of an ordinary consumer of the goods or services, and whether that creates a real and tangible danger of confusion. [TMM/Part26/6/6/4~1]\n\nThese examination directions are Manual practice; the statutory test remains section 10."
